  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-09-07T08:08:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "1131a514-bff8-41d5-801d-bb64fccf3357",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 070808\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n108 AM PDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Drier conditions expected much of the week with warming \n   temperatures expected.\n\n*  Elevated south-southwesterly wind gusts expected on Thursday and \n   Friday across the region.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Sunday.\n\nDry conditions are expected through much of the week as the current \nupper level trough lifts out and high pressure sets up over the \nregion. This area of high pressure will center overhead, which is \ntoo far west for monsoonal moisture to return to the region, which \nwill limit thunderstorm chances this week. Isolated showers and \nthunderstorms are possible on the Sierra crest Wednesday and \nThursday afternoon as upslope flow squeezes out what little moisture \nmay be in the atmosphere, but little is expected to make it over the \nridge. With building heights aloft, temperatures will quickly warm, \nclimbing back to above-normal levels by Wednesday. The heat will \npeak Thursday or Friday as HeatRisk reaches Major in desert valleys. \nAfter Thursday, temperatures should begin to moderate, falling \ncloser to normal levels for the weekend, with HeatRisk dropping to \nModerate or Minor in many locations.  \n\nAn upper level trough is currently expected to push through the \nPacific Northwest toward the end of the week. This is currently \nexpected to remain too far north to produce precipitation in the \nlocal area. As the trough knocks down the established ridge over \nover the region, the pressure gradient should increase and will \nresult in gusty south to southwest winds. Currently the only area \nwith over a 50% chance for potentially damaging winds is in the \nWestern Mojave Desert with gusts up to 40 MPH. The other area of \nconcern will be the Colorado River Valley where southerly gusts and \nbuilding waves could impact boaters. With this being a late-week \nsystem, changes to the forecast are likely, so check back if \nplanning to spend time outdoors.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds  \nearly in the forecast period tonight will vary between 150-190 \ntrue, with speeds around 8KT before becoming light and variable. \nThereafter, winds will follow typical daily directional patterns, \nshifting to the east-northeast by mid-morning, then veering to the\nsouthwest during the afternoon with gusts to 20-25KT expected \nthrough around sunset. VFR conditions prevail with no \noperationally significant cloud cover.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Lingering light rain\nshowers mainly south of I-15 will continue dissipating tonight, \nwith no impacts anticipated. Ceilings gradually improve overnight,\nwith winds expected to generally follow typical daily directional\npatterns the next 24 hours. Southwesterly winds will gust to \naround 20-25KT across the Las Vegas Valley Monday afternoon, with \nsimilar westerly gusts across the western Mojave Desert/KDAG \nMonday afternoon through late evening. Elsewhere, winds are \nexpected to remain around 10KT or less. VFR conditions will \nprevail through the forecast period.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
